# Performance & Clean Code

## Database (N+1 and queries)

- **Always eager load** relationships used in loops or views: `->with(['user', 'quotation.sourcingRequest'])`.
- **Avoid** loading full models when only IDs/counts are needed: use `->select()`, `->exists()`, `->count()`.
- **Chunk** large iterations: `Model::chunk(100, fn ($items) => ...)` instead of `Model::all()`.
- **Use indexes** for filtered/sorted columns; avoid `SELECT *` in repositories when only a few columns are needed.

```php
// BAD: N+1
foreach (SourcingOrder::all() as $order) {
    echo $order->user->name; // extra query per order
}

// GOOD
SourcingOrder::with('user')->get()->each(fn ($o) => $o->user->name);
```

## Caching

- Cache expensive computations and external API results with a TTL.
- Use cache keys that include entity ID/version to avoid stale data: `"order:{$id}:v{$updated_at}"`.
- Prefer `Cache::remember()` for read-through; invalidate on write (e.g. when order is updated).

## Collections and loops

- Prefer collection methods over raw loops when readable: `->filter()`, `->map()`, `->pluck()`.
- Avoid repeated array searches: build a keyed array or use `->keyBy('id')` for lookups.
- Do not load large collections into memory when a cursor/chunk is enough.

## Queue and async

- Put heavy work (emails, PDFs, external APIs, sheet sync) in jobs; keep HTTP requests fast.
- Use `dispatch()->afterResponse()` or queue from controllers so the response is not delayed.
- Limit job payload size; pass IDs and re-query in the job instead of serializing huge models.

## General

- Prefer early returns to reduce nesting and improve readability.
- Keep methods small; extract to private methods or services when logic grows.
- Use `readonly` and strict types to avoid accidental mutations and improve clarity.
